[section:introduction Introduction]

[heading What is Boost.VirtualSerial?]

Boost.VirtualSerial provides a serial stream with the same interface as
`boost::asio::serial_port`, but with a transport that can be selected at
compile time or at run time.  The available transports are:

[variablelist
    [[physical port][A real operating system serial port, implemented with
        `boost::asio::serial_port`.]]
    [[RFC 2217][A serial port published by a Telnet Com Port Control access
        server; baud rate, framing and flow control are applied on the
        remote device.]]
    [[raw TCP][A serial device exposed as a plain TCP byte stream.]]
    [[UDP][A serial device exposed as connected datagrams.]]
    [[mock fixture][A deterministic in-memory port with data injection,
        fault injection and a virtual clock.]]
]

[heading Why not use `boost::asio::serial_port` directly?]

`boost::asio::serial_port` is a concrete type: it owns its native handle and
there is no supported way to substitute the transport.  As a consequence,
tests require real hardware, remote serial consoles cannot be reached with
the same code, and error paths such as framing errors, overruns and cable
pulls cannot be reproduced on demand.

Boost.VirtualSerial keeps the Asio programming model - the same
`read_some`/`write_some`/`async_read_some`/`async_write_some` quartet, the
same `serial_port_base` option objects, the same executor semantics - and
moves the transport behind a seam.

[endsect]


[section:quickstart Quick start]

[heading Physical port]

[c++]

    #include <boost/virtual_serial.hpp>
    #include <boost/asio/io_context.hpp>
    #include <boost/asio/read.hpp>
    #include <boost/asio/write.hpp>

    int main()
    {
        boost::asio::io_context io;
        boost::virtual_serial::basic_serial_port<
            boost::virtual_serial::physical_port> port(io.get_executor());

        port.set_option(boost::asio::serial_port_base::baud_rate(115200));
        port.set_option(boost::virtual_serial::read_timeout(
            std::chrono::milliseconds(500)));

        port.open("COM3");

        boost::asio::write(port, boost::asio::buffer("AT\r\n", 4));

        char reply[128];
        boost::system::error_code ec;
        const std::size_t n = port.read_some(boost::asio::buffer(reply), ec);
    }

[heading Selection by URI]

[c++]

    auto port = boost::virtual_serial::make_serial_port(
        io.get_executor(), "rfc2217://lab-host:7001?baud=115200");

`make_serial_port` returns a `boost::virtual_serial::any_serial_port`, which
accepts exactly the same operations as the compile-time form above.

[endsect]


[section:mock Testing with the mock fixture]

The mock backend is a complete Asio stream backed by memory.  It is used
exactly like a physical port:

[c++]

    auto mock = boost::virtual_serial::make_mock_port(io.get_executor());
    boost::virtual_serial::any_serial_port port(io.get_executor(), mock);

    mock->set_write_handler([mock](const unsigned char*, std::size_t) {
        mock->inject_read("OK\r\n");
    });

    port.open("at-modem");
    boost::asio::write(port, boost::asio::buffer("AT\r\n", 4));

[heading Data injection]

[variablelist
    [[`inject_read`][Push raw bytes or a string into the receive path.]]
    [[`inject_line`][Push a line followed by CRLF.]]
    [[`inject_eof`][Report end of stream once buffered data is consumed.]]
]

[heading Fault injection]

[variablelist
    [[`inject_framing_error`][The next read reports
        `boost::virtual_serial::error::framing_error`.]]
    [[`inject_parity_error`][The next read reports a parity error.]]
    [[`inject_overrun`][The next read reports a FIFO overrun.]]
    [[`inject_break`][The next read reports a break condition.]]
    [[`inject_corrupt_byte`][Replaces the next byte and reports a line
        error.]]
    [[`inject_hangup`][Every subsequent read reports a hangup; the port stays
        open.]]
    [[`inject_disconnect`][Drops the port and fails reads with
        `error::not_connected`.]]
    [[`inject_error`][Reports an arbitrary error on the next read.]]
    [[`inject_write_error`][Fails the next write.]]
]

Buffer overflows are modelled with a bounded FIFO:

[c++]

    mock->set_read_capacity(64);
    mock->set_overflow_policy(
        boost::virtual_serial::overflow_policy::drop_oldest);

[heading Clocked data]

With pacing enabled, every injected byte receives a due time derived from the
configured framing, and becomes readable only when the virtual clock reaches
it.  The clock is advanced by the test, never by the scheduler:

[c++]

    mock->set_pacing(true);
    mock->inject_read("GO");

    mock->step();                              // deliver exactly one byte
    mock->advance(mock->byte_duration());      // deliver the next one
    mock->flush_input();                       // deliver everything

[endsect]


[section:backends Backend reference]

[table Backends
    [[Backend][Header][Transport][Serial options]]
    [[`physical_port`][`backends/physical_port.hpp`][`serial_port`][applied to the UART]]
    [[`mock_port`][`backends/mock_port.hpp`][memory][stored and validated]]
    [[`rfc2217_port`][`backends/rfc2217_port.hpp`][TCP + Telnet 44][applied on the server]]
    [[`tcp_bridge_port`][`backends/tcp_bridge_port.hpp`][TCP][stored only]]
    [[`udp_bridge_port`][`backends/udp_bridge_port.hpp`][UDP][stored only]]
]

[heading RFC 2217 client]

`rfc2217_port` performs Telnet option 44 negotiation, sends
`SET-BAUDRATE`, `SET-DATASIZE`, `SET-PARITY`, `SET-STOPSIZE` and
`SET-CONTROL` in the recommended order, requests line state notifications
for framing, parity, overrun and break errors, and translates
`NOTIFY-LINESTATE` into the same error values as the other backends.  Data is
quoted and unquoted according to the Telnet IAC rules.

[endsect]


[section:options Options and timeouts]

The five Asio serial options are accepted directly:

[c++]

    port.set_option(boost::asio::serial_port_base::baud_rate(115200));
    port.set_option(boost::asio::serial_port_base::character_size(8));
    port.set_option(boost::asio::serial_port_base::parity(
        boost::asio::serial_port_base::parity::even));
    port.set_option(boost::asio::serial_port_base::stop_bits(
        boost::asio::serial_port_base::stop_bits::one));
    port.set_option(boost::asio::serial_port_base::flow_control(
        boost::asio::serial_port_base::flow_control::none));

In addition, two front-end options are enforced for every backend:

[c++]

    port.set_option(boost::virtual_serial::read_timeout(
        std::chrono::milliseconds(250)));
    port.set_option(boost::virtual_serial::write_timeout(
        std::chrono::milliseconds(250)));

An unknown option type reports
`boost::virtual_serial::error::unsupported_option` at run time instead of
failing to compile.

[endsect]

[section:coroutines Coroutines (C++20)]

Every port models the Asio async stream concepts, so coroutine tokens compose
directly: [^co_await port.async_read_some(buffers, boost::asio::use_awaitable)]
works without a wrapper.  The optional
[^boost/virtual_serial/awaitable.hpp] header adds the helpers [^read_some],
[^read_exactly], [^write_all] and [^read_until], which run on the port's
executor; a timed-out read throws [^boost::system::system_error] with
[^virtual_serial::error::timeout].  The helpers are compiled only when
[^BOOST_VIRTUAL_SERIAL_HAS_COROUTINES] is set, so C++14 builds are unaffected.

[endsect]

[section:concepts Concepts (C++20)]

[^boost/virtual_serial/concepts.hpp] defines [^AsyncSerialStream],
[^SerialBackend] and [^SerialOption].  Every front end and backend satisfies
the first; the second adds the backend-only operations; the third accepts the
forwarded option objects.  The concepts are optional diagnostics and are
available only when [^BOOST_VIRTUAL_SERIAL_HAS_CONCEPTS] is set.

[endsect]


[section:errors Error handling]

[variablelist
    [[`error::not_connected`][The port is closed, or the peer went away.]]
    [[`error::already_open`][`open` was called on an open port.]]
    [[`error::bad_target`][The target string could not be parsed.]]
    [[`error::invalid_option`][The option value is out of range.]]
    [[`error::unsupported_option`][The backend cannot represent the option.]]
    [[`error::framing_error`][UART framing error.]]
    [[`error::parity_error`][UART parity error.]]
    [[`error::overrun`][Receive buffer overrun.]]
    [[`error::break_detected`][Break condition on the line.]]
    [[`error::hangup`][Carrier lost.]]
    [[`error::timeout`][Front-end read or write timeout.]]
    [[`error::protocol_error`][Malformed transport framing.]]
    [[`error::queue_full`][The mock FIFO rejected injected bytes.]]
    [[`error::aborted`][The operation was cancelled.]]
    [[`error::not_supported`][The backend does not implement the feature.]]
]

`boost::system::is_error_code_enum` is specialised for
`boost::virtual_serial::error`, so the values convert to and compare with
`boost::system::error_code`.

[endsect]


[section:threading Threading]

Distinct port objects may be used from different threads.  A port shared
between threads must be serialised, typically with a strand, exactly as with
`boost::asio::serial_port`.  Completion handlers are invoked through their
associated executor with `boost::asio::post`, so a handler bound to a strand
stays on the strand and a handler bound to `use_future` produces a future.

[endsect]


[section:reference Reference]

[heading `basic_serial_port`]

[c++]

    template <class Backend>
    class basic_serial_port
    {
    public:
        using executor_type = typename Backend::executor_type;

        basic_serial_port(executor_type, std::shared_ptr<Backend>);
        explicit basic_serial_port(executor_type);

        executor_type get_executor() const noexcept;
        bool is_open() const noexcept;
        backend_kind backend() const noexcept;
        serial_config config() const;

        void open(const std::string&);
        void open(const std::string&, boost::system::error_code&);
        void close();
        void close(boost::system::error_code&);
        void cancel();
        void cancel(boost::system::error_code&);

        template <class MutableBufferSequence>
        std::size_t read_some(const MutableBufferSequence&,
            boost::system::error_code&);

        template <class ConstBufferSequence>
        std::size_t write_some(const ConstBufferSequence&,
            boost::system::error_code&);

        template <class MutableBufferSequence, class CompletionToken>
        auto async_read_some(const MutableBufferSequence&, CompletionToken&&);

        template <class ConstBufferSequence, class CompletionToken>
        auto async_write_some(const ConstBufferSequence&, CompletionToken&&);

        template <class Option> void set_option(const Option&);
        template <class Option> void get_option(Option&) const;
    };

[heading `any_serial_port`]

[c++]

    using any_serial_port = basic_serial_port<detail::erased_backend>;

[heading Factory functions]

[c++]

    std::shared_ptr<mock_port> make_mock_port(
        const boost::asio::any_io_executor&,
        const serial_config& = serial_config());

    any_serial_port make_serial_port(
        const boost::asio::any_io_executor&, const serial_config&);

    any_serial_port make_serial_port(
        const boost::asio::any_io_executor&, const std::string& uri);

[endsect]


[section:tests Tests]

The test suite covers the option model, URI grammar, buffer normalisation,
the Telnet/RFC 2217 codec, every mock injection mode, the type-erased front
end and end-to-end loopback tests against in-process TCP, UDP and RFC 2217
servers.  Every public header is also compiled as C++14, and the C++20
concept conformance of every front end and backend is asserted separately.

An asynchronous contract suite asserts the Asio stream concepts at compile
time and proves at run time that an outstanding operation keeps
[^io_context] alive, that handlers are never invoked inline, run exactly once
and on their associated executor, that a strand serialises handlers across
threads, and that [^async_read], [^async_write], [^async_read_until] and a
user-written [^async_compose] operation work on the type-erased port.

Two further suites exist for continuous integration: a serial-mode matrix that
drives every backend in both directions with fault injection and recovery, and
a physical-loopback suite that opens a real serial pair (created with
[^tools/pty_loopback.py] on Linux and macOS).  Coverage is measured with LLVM
profile data and gated at 90% of lines and 80% of branches on Linux, macOS and
Windows.

[endsect]

[section:performance Performance]

The [^perf] directory contains an opt-in benchmark suite (enable it with
[^BOOST_VIRTUAL_SERIAL_BUILD_PERF]=ON) that measures synchronous and
asynchronous throughput, operation rate, latency percentiles, the cost of
type erasure, TCP loopback throughput and round-trip time, the Telnet codec and
allocations per asynchronous operation.  Results are JSON, rendered and gated by
[^tools/perf_report.py]; continuous integration publishes a table per platform
and enforces [^perf/floors.json].

[endsect]


[section:acknowledgements Acknowledgements]

The Asio stream interface, the executor model and the serial option
vocabulary are the work of the Boost.Asio author and maintainers.  The RFC
2217 implementation follows RFC 2217 by G. Clark.

[endsect]
